Rast <rast2@hotmail.com> writes:
> If it isn't cheating for a person to do something, it isn't cheating for 
> your computer or well-trained monkey to do it.  My view will probably 
> change if AI ever advances to the "smart as humans" stage.

Consider a hypothetical wrapper program that sits between your
terminal and vanilla textmode nethack, and mediates your interaction
with the game.  Further imagine that this wrapper is programmed to ask
"do you really want to?" questions when the player tries to take some
action which is likely a mistake.

Specific examples could include:
   o- whacking a peaceful with Stormy
   o- putting a /oCancel in a BoH
   o- spelling Elberteh wrong[1]
   o- praying when your prayer timeout hasn't expired
   o- not stopping eating when continuing will kill you
   o- stepping on a known trap

A human _could_ avoid all these things with sufficient care and turn-
counting. Yet these are all things that real players get wrong; having
a program to automatically prevent them would change the character of
gameplay appreciably.

Now consider a wrapper which would highlight squares on the map where
you've seen some creature (including self, pets or monsters) step, and
(differently) highlight squares you've searched. You could use this
information to greatly reduce the chances of tripping over a hidden
trap.

A human, given good enough memory (or incredible patience for
note-taking), _could_ in theory do this. Yet it is very hard for
humans, very easy for a computer, and could potentially make the game
significantly easier for Joe Random Hacker.

I'd regard such things as (mild) cheating, at least for purposes like
bragging rights, YAAPs, submitting bones to Hearse, tournament play
and scores on a multi-user system.

[1]-- A province in Canada, I think. 


